# Flow API design

The algorithms of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation.

### Fearless facts

Fearless has two kinds of errors: deterministic and non deterministic (like stack overflow).
They both use an imm Info object instead of an arbitrary error object.
`mut CapTry`(from `mut System`) and `Try` both uphold strong exception safety; the only difference is whether a non deterministic error can be caught at all: `Try#{..}` catches only deterministic errors thrown by `..`, `myCapTry#{..}` all of the errors thrown by `..`.
`Repr` and `IsoPod` both offer reads and mutation in place (`look`, `mutate`);
an `IsoPod` can also be opened once, giving its `iso` back and staying empty from then on.
`Reprs#` and `IsoPods#` take an `iso T`, so two distinct `Repr`s or `IsoPod`s share no mutable object.
`mutH` and `readH` are the 'hygienic' capabilities; a `mutH` value is a `readH` value.
A hygienic binding can be called and passed on as an argument, but never stored, returned, sent downstream or captured by any literal.
A `mut` method called on a hygienic receiver takes only `iso` and `imm` arguments, and a `mut` or `read` result comes back `mutH` or `readH`.

### Properties every part of the language respects

**Observable Determinism:**\
A method with no mut parameters (including the receiver) returns structurally equivalent results for structurally equivalent input. "Results" includes deterministic errors and non termination: the result may be non termination or a structurally equivalent thrown Info.

Determinism holds because every method of an object capability doing something non deterministic is a mut method: with no mut in input, the method can call no such method.

**Immutability:**\
An imm reference refers to an object whose ROG is deeply unmodifiable: nothing in that ROG will ever change again.

**read:**\
A read reference can not be used to mutate an object or anything in its ROG.
If a method has no mut parameters, any read parameter is also deeply unmodifiable for the duration of the call, since no mut reference to its ROG can be in scope. This allows us to reason inductively about deterministic parallelism: if a read binding `r` is in scope, there will be no other active parallel execution with a mut reference to any part of `r`'s ROG.

**iso:**\
An iso reference refers to an object whose (mutable) ROG is encapsulated under that single reference. The ROG may share imm objects, which are referentially transparent, but every mutable object in it would be garbage collectable if that iso reference were lost.

**Strong exception safety:**\
The body of a try either completes successfully or leaves the state observable outside of the try unmodified. Enforced by the type system: `Try#[R](try: read F[R]): mut Action[R]` and `Try#[T,R](data: iso T, try: read F[iso T,R]): mut Action[R]`. A `read` lambda can not mutate what it captures, so the body mutates only what it creates and `data`. The `Action` holds the value or the `Info`.

Example: with `rs` of type `mut List[mut Repr[T]]`,
`Try#{.. rs.flow(ParRepr#).map{r -> r.mutate{...}}.list}`
compiles only if `rs` is created inside the `{..` part or passed as `data`, not captured by the `Try` lambda.
Thus running that flow with data parallelism is correct: even if the code executed as part of the map stage throws on the element at index 3 of `rs`, no non determinism is observable, since all of ROG(rs) is garbage collectable when the error is caught.

**Identity Transparency:**\
Without using mut methods of capability objects, we can not distinguish whether two references are identity equal or just structurally equivalent.\
No `_Magic!` method breaks Identity Transparency. Identity Transparency is needed for Determinism and caching: otherwise one could call something twice and tell re-computation from a cached result, violating Determinism of an outer expression that internally uses caching (and tests for identity).

### Terms

- 'throw'. Not: raise.
- 'error': the still uncaught Info object. Not 'exception'.
- 'catch' is what `Try#` does; 'capture' is what a lambda does.
- 'garbage collectable': an object no reference leads to. Not: unreachable.
- 'structurally equivalent'. Not: structurally identical, equal, identical, same.
- 'identity equal': Java ==; do not use language confusing == and structurally equivalent.
- 'reach': only about the ROG: an object reaches what is in its ROG.
- 'desugar/desugaring'. Not: sugar, sugaring, semantic reduction, reduce, implemented on top of, encoded on top of.
- 'other approaches': flow and stream libraries of other languages. Not: other libraries, other systems, similar stream APIs.

Flow expressions:
- 'source': a call returning a fresh `mut Flow`.
- 'stage': a flow method returning another flow.
- 'terminal': a flow method ending the flow and returning something that is not a flow.
- 'whole flow expression': a source, its stages and its terminal. Not: this flow.
- 'chain': what the flow object records of the whole flow expression: the source, its mode, its hints and each stage/terminal with its arguments. Not: pipeline; pipeline is only pipeline parallelism.
- 'shape': the chain without the values of its arguments: the sequence of the stages/terminals.
- 'executed as part of the xxx stage' (for example the map stage): user code, provided via a lambda passed to that stage. Do not use general terms like "earlier", "first", "later" etc.
- 'core method': a method with a direct implementation; every other flow method desugars to core methods.
- use 'stage/terminal' to mean either a stage or a terminal. Do not use 'operation'.

Elements and order:
- 'flow order': the order in which one stage/terminal receives the elements. Not: source order, index order.
- 'index': the place of an element in the source, as `j` in `ej`; for example after a `filter`, `flatMap` or `sort` the index of an element is not its place in the flow order.
- 'send downstream', 'receives': a stage sends elements downstream, the next stage/terminal receives them. Not: pull, push, arrive, reach, move, go past, come out, hand on, deliver, release.
- 'trickle': the elements go through the stages one at a time; the work on the element processed in flow order `j` comes before any work on the element at flow order `j+1`, as far as it can be observed.
- 'materialise': collect elements into a list inside the whole flow expression.
- 'expansion': the `ToFlow` the lambda of `flatMap` or `flatMapMut` returns for one element.

Kinds of stages/terminals:
- 'elementwise' stage: `map`, `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`, `peek`, `mapFilter`, `readOnly`.
- 'streaming stateful' stage: `limit`, `scan`, `actor`, `actorMut`, map and peek with context, `distinct`.
- 'fence': a stage/terminal that sends nothing downstream, and lets no error of its own propagate, until every stage before it has finished with every element: the stages `allToAll`, `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`, `distinctMut` and `++`, and the terminal `for`. Not: fence operation, barrier.
- 'consuming terminal': `list`, `eList`, `set`, `eSet`, `mapping`, `for`, `forEach`, `fold`, `foldAcc`, `size`, `isEmpty`, `get`, `getOpt`, `tryGet`, `tryGetOpt`, `join`, `sum`, `last`, `with`, `withBoth`, `withAll`. A consuming terminal never stops receiving: it receives every element the stage before it sends downstream.
- 'utility method': a flow method that is neither a stage nor a terminal: it ends the flow expression with `.list` and returns what its lambdas compute from that list, possibly a flow. `let` is the only one.
- 'short-circuit terminal': `first`, `any`, `all`, `none`, `findFirst`, `findMap`. They can decide before the end, leaving the elements after the decision in flow order never processed.

Multi flows:
- 'multi flow': a `BiFlow`, `TriFlow` or `QuadFlow`. A multi flow is not a flow: it is a flow factory, as `Flows` is. Not: zipped flow, tuple flow.
- 'channel': one of the two, three or four lists of elements a multi flow holds. The element at index `i` of a channel goes with the element at index `i` of every other channel.
- 'merge': `getMerge`, `tryGetMerge`, `merge` or `mergeOpts`: a multi flow method returning a fresh `mut Flow`, directly or inside an `Action` or an `Opt`. A merge is the source of that flow.

Results and errors:
- 'returns': includes the result value, deterministic errors and non termination. Do not use 'produces' 'evaluate into'.
- 'completes successfully': the sub set of 'returns' including only the result value.
- 'held': an error not yet allowed to propagate out of the flow expression. Not: kept on hold, wait.
- 'discard': drop a held error or a computed result for good. Not: never surfaces, never reported.
- 'cancel': stop a running call from outside, through `Yield#`. Not: terminate; terminate is only used in "does not terminate".

Strategies:
- 'mode': `Seq`, `ParImm`, `ParRead`, `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, recorded by the source and by each `.recoverFlow`. Not: execution mode, par mode.
- 'strategy': how the terminal runs the whole flow expression: the sequential strategy, data parallelism, pipeline parallelism, or a mix of them. Not: mode, shape, parallel shape.
- 'sequential strategy': runs the elements through the stages one at a time. Not: sequential execution, sequential run, sequential mode.
- 'hint': `.hintSequential` or `.hintParallel`, recorded in the chain to say which strategy the user prefers for the 'part' that holds the hint. A 'part' is a piece of the chain cut at each `.recoverFlow`: from the source or a `.recoverFlow` to the next `.recoverFlow` or the terminal. A hint changes nothing a flow expression returns. Not: annotation, option.
- 'data parallelism/fork-join': the elements are split into chunks; a task runs one chunk through a sequence of stages; workers execute tasks.
- 'worker'. Not: thread, core.
- 'data parallel region': consecutive stages (could be a single one) running with data parallelism. Not: parallel region, section.
- 'pipeline parallelism/nodes': stages run in nodes; a node receives elements in flow order and sends its output to the next node. Elements can be in chunks or not.
- 'node': one part of pipeline parallelism, as an actor in actor systems. Not: actor, worker; actor is only the `actor` stage, do not use 'actor' as a worker entity to avoid confusion.
- 'speculative call': a call a parallel strategy makes before it is known whether the sequential strategy makes that call.
- 'speculation calls': the calls that could be made by a flow expression in a parallel strategy, but that the same flow expression would not make in a sequential strategy.
- 'positive speculation calls': the calls that could be made by a flow expression that completes successfully in a parallel strategy, but that the same flow expression would not make in a sequential strategy.
- 'negative speculation calls': speculation calls that are not positive speculation calls. That is, calls that parallelism performs early that would be eventually executed if the flow expression completes successfully, but that could be prevented by an error or non termination of a call the sequential strategy makes before them.
- 'speculation driver': a stage or terminal that can decide that the elements after some element are never processed, so that running the stages before it ahead of that decision makes positive speculation calls: `limit`, an `actor` or `actorMut` returning `Break#`, and the short-circuit terminals. In `.map a .sort .map b .findFirst c`, `.findFirst c`.
- 'speculating stage': a stage that is not a fence, with no fence between that stage and a speculation driver after it; also a short-circuit terminal with a lambda, for that lambda. Running a speculating stage in parallel makes positive speculation calls. In `.map a .sort .map b .findFirst c`, `.map b` and `.findFirst c` are speculating stages; `.map a` is not one, since `.sort` is a fence.

### The two kinds of parallelism

**Data parallelism/fork-join:**\
The iconic map reduce set up: the elements of `a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list` are divided into chunks, one task per chunk; the workers execute the tasks.
Each task conceptually applies `f1#e |> {x->f2#x .thenDo{acc.add x}}` to each element `e` of its chunk, with an accumulator `acc` that preserves the flow order.

**Pipeline parallelism/nodes:**\
What happens in actor systems: `a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list` is made of 3 nodes.
The `map` node receives elements from the flow (in chunks or not), applies f1 and sends them to the mailbox of the `filter` node, which applies f2 and sends the survivors to the `list` node, building the list. The 3 nodes run in parallel, and each can freely update local mutable data, since it receives its elements one at a time, in flow order.

Pipeline parallelism applies in plenty of cases where data parallelism does not, and it is more efficient when code locality is crucial: the same node runs the same code over and over, and if that code fits in cache this may beat data parallelism, especially when the whole flow expression (not broken into chunks) would not fit in cache.

### How the semantics is given

The semantics is given by multiple descriptions, validated by checking that they all agree:
1. the simple composition of all the stages and the terminal;
2. desugaring all flow methods into other methods, until a few core methods remain with an un-optimised but trivially correct direct implementation in pseudocode;
3. examples of many thorny cases;
4. translation of full flow expressions into traditional imperative statements/pseudocode.

### Examples and pseudocode

Unless an example says otherwise, `l` is `Lists#(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7)`, with `ej` its element at index `j`, and `f` and `g` are of type `read F[Nat,Nat]`.

Example pseudocode with `ps` a `List[Person]`, `ps.flow.map{::.name}.filter{::.size > 3}.list` is
```
EList[Str] res= EList#;
for(Person p: ps){
  Str name= p.name;
  if(!(name.size > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(name);
  }
return res.list;
```
The pseudocode uses a few primitives:
- `m : Mailbox[T]`: elements in flow order, possibly followed by one error. `for(T x: m){..}` waits for each element in turn, and throws the error in its place; `m.join` waits for the end of `m`, and throws its error if any. `m.list` and `m.first` are sequential methods of `Mailbox`.
- `fork(Range c: Chunks#n){..}` splits the indexes `0 ..< n` into chunks and starts one task per chunk, running the block on some worker. The block sends elements with `out.send(x)`. `fork` returns the mailbox of what the blocks send: chunk after chunk in index order, and in sending order inside one chunk. An error thrown by a block takes the place of the next element of its chunk and ends the mailbox: what the chunks after it send is discarded.
`fork(List[T] c: Chunks#m){..}` does the same over the elements of the mailbox `m`, forming each chunk as it receives its elements. An error met in `m` comes after what the chunks of the elements before it send, and ends the mailbox.
`node(T x: m){..}` over a mailbox or a list `m` processes the elements one at a time, in flow order, sending with `out.send(x)`, and returns the mailbox of what it sends; it may update the local variables declared just before it, which no other code uses. An error thrown by the block, or met in `m`, takes the place of the next element the node sends, and ends the mailbox of the node. All nodes in a pseudocode section start at the same time, so that they can work in parallel (as soon/as long as they all have elements).
- `Mailbox[T] feed= Mailbox#;` is an empty mailbox the code itself feeds, with `feed.send(x)` and `feed.close`.
- `IdentityCheck#rs` throws a non deterministic error when two elements of `rs` are identity equal.
- `KeySet#by` is a hash set placing its keys with the calls of `_KeepNew`; `s.add(x)` returns whether `x` was new.
- `Slots#n` holds an `Opt` per index, empty until a task sets it.
- when the pseudocode completes (return or error), it cancels through `Yield#` every task and node still running.

The flow expression above becomes, with data parallelism:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(ps.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    Str name= ps.get(j).name;
    if(!(name.size > 3)){ continue; }
    out.send(name);
    }
  }.list;//EList[T] res= EList#; for(T x: this){ res.add(x); } return res.list;
```
and with pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Str] names= node(Person p: ps){ out.send(p.name) };
return node(Str name:names){ if(name.size > 3){ out.send(name); }}.list;
```

## Current design

The source and the stages record what they are, in the chain; the terminal checks the chain and runs the whole flow expression.
`.limit 0` and a stage called on a flow already consumed throw where they are called, before the terminal.

