## How: the parallel strategies

### Sources and modes

The mode decides which strategies the terminal may pick:
- `Seq`: only the sequential strategy, over any elements.
- `ParRead`: the parallel strategies too, over `read` or `imm` elements.
- `ParImm`: as `ParRead`, and the source elements are `imm`, as the condition of `.forEach` and the speculative strategy of `.for` need.
- `ParRepr` over `mut Repr[T]` and `ParPod` over `mut IsoPod[T]`: the parallel strategies too, over elements each owning its mutable state.

The flow terminal may choose to use a parallel strategy only for a part with no `.hintSequential`.
The presence of `.hintParallel` further incentivizes the flow terminal to apply a parallel strategy.

### Misuse errors and the parallel strategies

In `Base:Offensive`, every strategy, the sequential strategy included, runs `checkMisuse` before any element is sent downstream; a parallel strategy may instead run the identity check of `checkPar` on the calling worker while the tasks run, provided the identity check completes before the result of any task is used.

No `imm` value can reach a `mut Repr` or `mut IsoPod`, so with the flag each task reaches no `Repr` or `IsoPod` but its own element; the lambda of `for` runs once every stage has finished. The backend marks the lambda objects carrying the flag with the `OnlyImmCapture` marker interface of the runtime (in Java), not visible in Fearless; when the lambdas are written in place, the flag is checked when compiling and leaves no code.

Over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, and in a chain of mode `Seq` containing a `.recoverFlow`, no strategy makes a positive speculation call executed as part of a stage/terminal at index `i` with `mutReach(i)`; `checkDecisions` throws on the shapes that would need one. Negative speculation calls are allowed over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, also while they still run after the whole flow expression has returned.

**Over `read` or `imm` elements:**\
The stage/terminal at index `i` runs over `read` or `imm` elements when `c.mode in {ParImm, ParRead} || !mutReach(i)` (**Misuse errors**).

### Duplicated `mut` elements

Every `mut` object a stage creates is reachable from nowhere else, until a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut`: they can send one `mut` object downstream as two elements, or as parts of two elements, like two pairs containing the same `Person`.
```
numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.flatMapMut{p -> Lists#(p, p)}.filter{..}.list
```
The lambda of `filter` is a `read F`: it can not mutate what it captured, but it can mutate its `mut` parameter, so two tasks running `filter` on the two copies would race on one `Person`.
No identity check can find the shared parts, so the elements a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut` sends downstream from the same input can not be given to different tasks, by any split into chunks, including one after a fence, until a `++` or a `distinct` as below. The list an `allToAll` with an arbitrary `f` returns may contain one `mut` object twice in the same way; `sort`, `max`, `min`, `sortDistinct`, `distinctMut` and `++` are recorded under their own names in the chain and return each element they received once, so they add no sharing, and a data parallel region can start after them unless the elements already shared `mut` objects.
`++` sends every element downstream as `read` or `imm`, so no stage after it can mutate an element, and elements sharing `mut` objects can go to different tasks.
`distinct`, though not a fence, does the same when only `limit`s stand between the `distinct` and the last `flatMapMut`, `actorMut` or fence before the `distinct`. `flatMap`, `scan` and `actor` never restore the split, though they send `read` or `imm` elements downstream.

### Every strategy returns what the sequential strategy returns

The calls of `by` are fixed by `_Sort`, `_Max`, `_Collapse` and `_KeepNew`, and every strategy makes the same calls.

**Holding:**\
In every parallel strategy, an error thrown by a call `c` is held until it is known whether the sequential strategy makes `c` and whether every call the sequential strategy makes before `c` completes successfully. The error propagates when both are known to be true, and is discarded when either is known to be false; if a call the sequential strategy makes before `c` does not terminate, the error is held forever.

**Decision points:**\
A decision point is a call of an `actor` or `actorMut` lambda returning `Break#`: after it, the actor receives no other element. `limit` desugars to such an `actorMut`, and the short-circuit terminals desugar to `.limit 1`.

**Termination:**\
The whole flow expression returns as soon as the sequential strategy would return: no terminal waits for a call the sequential strategy does not make, or makes after a call that throws.

**Non deterministic errors of speculative calls:**\
Non deterministic errors are discarded exactly as the deterministic ones: a stack overflow or an out of memory error thrown by a call the sequential strategy does not make never propagates, and one thrown at a greater index than a deterministic error never replaces it.

### A `Break#` while the call before it is still running

A stage after an `actor` or `actorMut` may return `Break#` on an element the actor sent downstream while the actor call that sent it is still running: the elements of a `flatMapMut` expansion after the one that fills a `limit`, or the code between two calls of `down.push#`.
That call is one the sequential strategy makes, and it completes: an element sent downstream after the break is dropped, the rest of the call runs, and what it throws or whether it terminates is part of what the whole flow expression returns.
No element is processed once the whole flow expression has returned.
With the actor ``{down, s, e -> Block#(down.push#(down, e), Error.msg`after`, Continue#)}`` before `.limit 1 .list`, the whole flow expression throws `after`.
Under pipeline parallelism the actor node may already be running the calls on the elements after it when the break is received: those calls are positive speculation calls, held as **Holding** says.
Where **Misuse errors and the parallel strategies** allows no positive speculation call, the actor node runs in lockstep with the stage after it.

### Cancellation: `Yield#`

`Yield#` is a cancellation point: in a speculative call whose result can no longer affect what the whole flow expression returns, it stops that call, and nothing the call computed is used; in every other call, under every strategy, it returns `Void` and does nothing. So it is not observable, and it can sit in any loop or recursion that may run for long, in the lambdas of a flow or in the methods they call. A speculative call that does not terminate releases its worker only through `Yield#`.
`Yield#` can stop a call anywhere, inside `Repr.mutate` and `IsoPod.mutate` as elsewhere.

### The calls of `by`

**Placement:**\
`distinct`, `distinctMut`, `mapping`, `set` and `eSet` place each key among the keys already present with the calls of `_KeepNew`. The placement makes its calls one at a time, in flow order, in every strategy.

**One merge tree for every strategy:**\
The pseudocode below writes `by.cmp(a, b)` for the comparison of the keys of `a` and `b`, computed once per element as `_Tree` does.
`_Sort#(vs, by, k)` calls `sort`, the 'merge tree': a stable merge sort over a tree fixed by the indexes alone, each merge keeping at most `k` elements:
```
List[Nat] sort(List[Nat] vs, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  if(vs.isEmpty){ return vs; }
  EList[List[Nat]] blocks= EList#;
  for(Nat j: vs.indexes){
    List[Nat] b= Lists#(vs.get(j));
    for(Nat n= j; n % 2 == 1; n= n / 2){ b= merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k); }
    blocks.push(b);
    }
  while(blocks.size > 1){
    List[Nat] b= blocks.pop;
    blocks.push(merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k));
    }
  return blocks.pop;
  }
List[Nat] merge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  EList[Nat] res= EList#;
  Nat i= 0;
  Nat j= 0;
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size && j < b.size){
    if(by.cmp(a.get(i), b.get(j)).isGt){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; continue; }
    res.add(a.get(i));
    i= i + 1;
    }
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size){ res.add(a.get(i)); i= i + 1; }
  while(res.size < k && j < b.size){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; }
  return res.list;
  }
```
Adding the element at index `j` runs one merge per trailing `1` in the binary digits of `j`: the binary counter. The left block is popped after the right one, so `a` is before `b` in flow order.
`_Max#(vs, by)`, the 'max tree', is the same tree with this merge in place of `merge`:
```
List[Nat] maxMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by){
  Ord o= by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0));
  if(o.isGt){ return a; }
  if(o.isLt){ return b; }
  return a ++ b;
  }
```
`MergeTree#(by, k)` makes the calls of `_Sort#`, with each merge running as a task of its own as soon as its two blocks are complete. `s.add(x)` adds `x` as the element at the next index; it never waits for a call of `by` and never throws. `s.list` merges the blocks left over from the right, waits for the merges in the order `_Sort#` makes them, and returns the result: if some calls of `by` throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one `_Sort#` makes before the others returns. `MaxTree#by` is the same for `_Max#`.

## Stages and terminals

### scan

`Flows#(1,2,3,4).scan(0, {acc, e -> acc + e}).list` returns `Lists#(1,3,6,10)`.

### flatMap takes a finished collection

```
Lists#(Lists#("aa","bbb"), Lists#("c","poison","dd"), Lists#("e"))
  .flow
  .flatMap{ss -> ss.flow
    .map{s -> s == "poison" ? {.then -> Error.msg`poison`; .else -> s}}.list}
  .limit 3
  .map{s -> s.size}
  .list
```
This throws: the lambda runs in full on the element at index 1, and meets `"poison"`.
With `.limit 2` it returns `Lists#(2, 3)`: the element at index 1 is never processed.

### actor and actorMut

The calls of the lambda of `actor`, `actorMut` and `scan` run one at a time, in flow order: their only parallel strategy is pipeline parallelism.

**Example for actor:**\
A sliding window of width 3:
```
Lists#(1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8).flow
  .actor[mut Var[List[Nat]], List[Nat]](Vars#[List[Nat]]{}, {down, s, e -> Block#(
    s.set(Trim#(s.get +> e, 3)),
    s.get.size == 3 .thenDo{ down.push#(down, s.get)},
    Continue#
    )})
  .map{..}
  .list
Trim: { #(w: List[Nat], width: Nat): List[Nat] ->
  w.size > width ? { .then -> w.subList(1); .else -> w } }
```
Here map sees `[1,2,3]`; `[2,3,4]`; ...`[6,7,8]`.

**Example for actorMut:**\
Heroes, `mut` elements, go through two rooms, each room the state of an `actorMut`, which sends downstream the heroes the room does not bury:
```
Room: {
  mut .fight(strength: Nat): Nat;
  mut .loot(strength: Nat): List[Item];
  mut .bury(grave: Opt[Grave]): Void;
  read .buried(name: Str): Bool;
  }
```

The hero and the room meet through imm values:
```
Visit: { #(room: mutH Room, h: mut Hero): Void -> Block#(
  h.wound(room.fight(h.strength)),
  h.carry(room.loot(h.carryCapacity)),//zero if dead already
  room.bury(h.grave)//empty Opt is still alive
  ) }
Survivor: { #(room: readH Room, h: mut Hero): mut Opt[mut Hero] ->
  room.buried(h.name) ?{ .then -> {}; .else -> Opts#h } }

heroes.flow
  .actorMut[Room, mut Hero](room1, {down, s, h -> Block#(Visit#(s, h), down.pushOpt#(down, Survivor#(s, h)), Continue#)})
  .actorMut[Room, mut Hero](room2, {down, s, h -> Block#(Visit#(s, h), down.pushOpt#(down, Survivor#(s, h)), Continue#)})
  .list
```

### max and min keep every tie

`max` and `min` send downstream every element tied for the maximum (minimum), in flow order.

`Maxes` is the readable version of `_Max#`.
`Maxes` returns the elements `_Max#` returns when every call of `by` completes successfully and respects the contract of an order, with the calls of a linear scan, one comparison per element after the first, instead of those of the max tree:
```
Maxes: { #[E:*,K](xs: mut List[E], by: OrderBy[imm E,K]): mut List[E] -> Block#
  .var[mut Opt[read Order[K]]] best = {{}}
  .var[mut EList[E]] run = {ELists#[E]}
  .var[Nat] i = {0}
  .loop{ i.get == (xs.size) ?[mut ControlFlow[mut List[E]]]{ .then -> Break#; .else -> Block#(
    MaxesStep#(best, run, by#(xs.get(i.get)), xs.get(i.get)),
    i.set(i.get + 1),
    Continue#) } }
  .return{ run.get.list } }
MaxesStep: { #[E:*,K](best: mut Var[mut Opt[read Order[K]]], run: mut Var[mut EList[E]], o: read Order[K], e: E): Void ->
  best.get.match{
    .empty -> Block#(best.set(Opts#o), run.get.add(e), Void);
    .some b -> (o <=> b)#{
      .lt -> Void;
      .eq -> Block#(run.get.add(e), Void);
      .gt -> Block#(best.set(Opts#o), run.set(ELists#[E]), run.get.add(e), Void) } } }
```

### distinct and distinctMut

With `by` ranking no two elements of `l` together, `f` throwing on `e5` and `g` throwing on `f#e2`, `l.flow.map f .distinct by .map g .list` throws the error of `g`, and `l.flow.map f .distinctMut by .map g .list` throws the error of `f`.
`.distinct by .filter p .first` runs the stages before `.distinct` on the elements up to the first one `.filter p` sends downstream; `.distinctMut by .filter p .first` runs them on every element.

### fold trickles

With `f` not terminating on `e1` and `h` throwing on its first call, `l.flow.map f .fold({0}, h)` throws the error of `h`.

### get and getOpt

```
Lists#(1,2,3).flow.map{x -> x == 3 ? {.then -> Error.msg`three`; .else -> x}}.get
```
throws `three`, not the error of `.tryGet` about the size.

### let

In `.let total = {xs -> ..} .flow(ParImm#) ..` of **example with let**, `total` names the result of `x`, and `.flow(ParImm#) ..` is called on the list `let` passes to `cont`.

### Multi flows

**From a map:**
```
people.flow{name, p -> Samples#(name, p.computeDNA)}
  .recoverFlow(ParImm#)
  .map{s -> s.matchScore(target)}
  .filter{m -> m.isClose}
  .list
```
with `people` a `mut Map[Str, mut Person]`, `computeDNA` returning an `imm DNA`, `Samples#` returning an `imm Sample`, and `target` an `imm DNA`.
The `map` stage and the `filter` stage can run with data parallelism.

**More channels:**\
Two maps aligned by insertion order:
```
left.flow.withBoth(right.flow)
  .getMerge{k1, p1, k2, p2 -> Couples#(k1, p1.computeDNA, k2, p2.computeDNA)}
  .recoverFlow(ParImm#)
  .map{c -> c.similarity}
  .list
```
with `left` and `right` two `mut Map[Str, mut Person]`, and `Couples#` returning an `imm Couple`.
The `map` stage can run with data parallelism.

## Examples

Each example states what the whole flow expression returns, then shows its strategies as pseudocode.

**example with a fence**\
```
l.flow.map f .sort by .filter {::>3}.first
```
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

Throws no deceptive shape error; with `.list` in place of `.first` it throws one.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by.cmp(...)` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, let `c` be the one the sorting process makes before the others: the whole flow expression returns what `c` returns.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index: the whole flow expression returns `Opts#(vk)` for the smallest `k` such that `vk > 3`, or the empty `Opt` when no such `k` exists.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
for(Nat v: _Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  return Opts#v;
  }
return Opt#;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
MergeTree[Nat] s= MergeTree#(by, l.size);
for(Nat v: m){ s.add(v); }
for(Nat v: s.list){
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  return Opts#v;
  }
return Opt#;
```
Pipeline parallelism is the same code, with `Mailbox[Nat] m= node(Nat e: l){ out.send(f#e); };`.

**example with elementwise work after a fence**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .map g .list`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by.cmp(...)` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, let `c` be the one the sorting process makes before the others: the whole flow expression returns what `c` returns.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index. If there is a smallest number `k` such that `g#vk` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `g#vk` returns. Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the list of `g#v0` to `g#v7`.

The calls of `g` never run in the tasks of `f`.

Running `g` while the sort runs needs the elements the fence receives to have a `read` or `imm` type. In
`numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.sort{p -> p.age}.map{p -> Block#(p.birthday, p)}.list`
the `map` after the fence mutates what `by` reads, and running it early would change the order.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: _Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){ res.add(g#v); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel, running `g` while the sort runs:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
List[Nat] vs= m.list;
MergeTree[Nat] s= MergeTree#({j -> by#(vs.get(j))}, vs.size);
for(Nat j: vs.indexes){ s.add(j); }
Slots[Nat] ws= Slots#(vs.size);
Mailbox[Void] gs= fork(Range c: Chunks#(vs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ ws.set(j, g#(vs.get(j))); }
  };
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat j: s.list){
  Opt[Nat] w= ws.get(j);
  if(w.isSome){ res.add(w!); continue; }
  res.add(g#(vs.get(j)));
  }
return res.list;
```
Nothing ever reads `gs`, so no error of `g` is held: a slot left empty, because its call threw, did not terminate or has not run yet, is computed again by the loop over the sorted indexes, which calls `g` in the flow order after the sort, as the sequential strategy does. By Determinism the call returns what the call in the task returned.

**example with a short-circuit terminal**\
`` l.flow.map{x -> x == 5 ? {.then -> Error.msg`five`; .else -> x}}.first ``

This code returns `Opts#(0)`; the error of the element at index 5 is never thrown.

The canonical form moves the `limit 1` of `.first` before the `map`: the map lambda runs only on the element at index `0`.

Sequential strategy:
```
for(Nat e: l.take(1)){
  if(e == 5){ Error.msg`five`; }
  return Opts#e;
  }
return Opt#;
```

**example with filter, under a short-circuit terminal**\
`l.flow.filter{x -> P#x}.any{x -> Q#x}`\
with `P` and `Q` of type `read F[Nat,Bool]`.

If there is a smallest index `i` such that `P#ei && {Q#ei}` does not complete successfully with `False`, the whole flow expression returns what `P#ei && {Q#ei}` returns; otherwise it returns `False`.

Sequential strategy:
```
for(Nat e: l){
  if(!P#e){ continue; }
  if(Q#e){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```
Data parallel, with positive speculation calls, allowed in `ParImm` and `ParRead`:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat e= l.get(j);
    if(!P#e){ continue; }
    if(!Q#e){ continue; }
    out.send(e);
    break;
    }
  }.first.isSome;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= node(Nat e: l){ if(P#e){ out.send(e); } };
for(Nat e: m){
  if(Q#e){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```

**example with filter, over ParRepr**\
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .size
```
If any two `Repr`s are identity equal, a non deterministic error is thrown.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` returns.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the number of indices `j` where `rj` holds more than `3` after being increased, and afterwards every `rj` in `rs` holds one more than `rj` held before the flow.

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityCheck#rs;
Nat n= 0;
for(mut Repr[Count[Nat]] r: rs){
  Nat x= r.mutate{i -> Block#(i++, i.get)};
  if(!(x > 3)){ continue; }
  n= n + 1;
  }
return n;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] ns= fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  Nat n= 0;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat x= rs.get(j).mutate{i -> Block#(i++, i.get)};
    if(!(x > 3)){ continue; }
    n= n + 1;
    }
  out.send(n);
  };
IdentityCheck#rs;
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat k: ns){ n= n + k; }
return n;
```

Consider the same example but with a short-circuit terminal like `.any` in place of `size`
