**example with `++` as a fence before positive speculation calls, over ParRepr**
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .map{r -> Block#(r.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}, r)}
  ++ List[mut Repr[Individual]]{}
  .any{r -> r.look{i -> i.fitness > 90}}
```
with `rs` a `mut List[mut Repr[Individual]]` and `Individual: { read .fitness: Nat; mut .evaluate: Void }`.

If any two `Repr`s are identity equal, a non deterministic error is thrown.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `rj.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}` returns.\
Otherwise every `Repr` in `rs` is evaluated; then, if there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.look{i -> i.fitness > 90}` does not complete successfully with `False`, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns; otherwise it returns `False`.

With the `++`, the `map` runs on every element in every strategy: a data parallel region with no positive speculation calls.
`++` is in `ToRead`: for the `any`, `mutReach` is false, `checkDecisions` throws nothing, and positive speculation calls are allowed (**Misuse errors and the parallel strategies**).

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityCheck#rs;
for(mut Repr[Individual] r: rs){ r.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}; }
for(mut Repr[Individual] r: rs){
  if(r.look{i -> i.fitness > 90}){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```
Data parallel, with positive speculation calls after the fence:
```
Mailbox[Void] done= fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ rs.get(j).mutate{i -> i.evaluate}; }
  };
IdentityCheck#rs;
done.join;
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    if(!rs.get(j).look{i -> i.fitness > 90}){ continue; }
    out.send(j);
    break;
    }
  }.first.isSome;
```

**example with size**\
`` l.flow.map{x -> Error.msg`boom`}.size ``

This code throws. In other approaches this may return the size without running the lambda, since the size is known.
`l.flow` records mode `ParImm`, so this throws a deceptive shape error; the pseudocode below is what runs under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`.

Sequential strategy:
```
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat x= Error.msg`boom`;
  n= n + 1;
  }
return n;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] ns= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  Nat n= 0;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat x= Error.msg`boom`;
    n= n + 1;
    }
  out.send(n);
  };
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat k: ns){ n= n + k; }
return n;
```
`x` is never read, but the call computing it stays, since it may throw or not terminate. No list is built: `.size` desugars to `.list.size`, but that list is not observable.

**example with limit**\
`l.flow.limit 3 .map f .map g .list`

If there is a smallest index `j` among `0`, `1` and `2` so that the expression `g#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `g#(f#ej)` returns. Otherwise the whole flow expression returns `Lists#(g#(f#e0), g#(f#e1), g#(f#e2))`.

The result does not depend on `f#ej` for any `j` above `2`, so an error thrown by `f#e5` or `g#(f#e5)` is discarded.\
In other approaches a parallel strategy may non deterministically let that error propagate.

The canonical form of this example is `l.flow.limit 3 .map{x -> g#(f#x)}.list`: the limit becomes part of the source, and the pseudocode is generated from what is left. `l.flow.map f .limit 3 .map g .list` throws a deceptive shape error, and under `.suppressMisuseExceptions` has the same canonical form. `l.take(3)` is the list of the first `3` elements of `l`, or all of them when `l` is shorter.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l.take(3)){ res.add(g#(f#e)); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size.min(3))){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(g#(f#(l.get(j)))); }
  }.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= node(Nat e: l.take(3)){ out.send(f#e); };
return node(Nat v: m){ out.send(g#v); }.list;
```
No strategy makes positive speculation calls: no element after index `2` is ever processed.

**example with for**\
`l.flow.map{_ -> v.get} .for{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}`\
with `v` a fresh `mut Var[Nat]` holding `0`, and `acc` a fresh `mut EList[Nat]`.

The whole flow expression returns `Void`, and afterwards `acc` holds eight times `0` and `v` holds `8`.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ xs.add(v.get); }
for(Nat x: xs){
  acc.add(x);
  v.set(v.get + 1);
  }
return Void;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(v.get); }
  };
List[Nat] xs= m.list;
for(Nat x: xs){
  acc.add(x);
  v.set(v.get + 1);
  }
return Void;
```
With `{_ -> 0}` in place of `{_ -> v.get}` the `map` lambda carries the onlyImmCapture flag, the `for` lambda runs in a node as it receives the elements, and `xs` is not needed:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(0); }
  };
Mailbox[Nat] feed= Mailbox#;
Opt[Info] held= Opt#;
Mailbox[Void] done= node(Nat x: feed){
  if(held.isSome){ return; }
  try{ acc.add(x); v.set(v.get + 1); }catch(Info i){ held= Opts#i; }
  };
for(Nat x: m){ feed.send(x); }
feed.close;
done.join;
if(held.isSome){ throw held!; }
return Void;
```

**example with forEach**\
`l.flow.map a .forEach b`\
with `a` a `read F[Nat,Nat]` carrying the onlyImmCapture flag, and `b` a `mut MF[Nat,Void]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `a#ej` or `b#(a#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what the first of the two that does returns. Otherwise it returns `Void`, after calling `b` on every `a#ej` in flow order.
With `b` not terminating on `a#e1` and `a` throwing on `e2`, it never returns, where `l.flow.map a .for b` throws the error of `a#e2`.

Sequential strategy:
```
for(Nat e: l){ b#(a#e); }
return Void;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(a#(l.get(j))); }
  };
for(Nat x: m){ b#x; }
return Void;
```

**example with let**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .let[Nat, mut List[Nat]] total = {xs -> xs.seqFlow.sum 0}
  .flow(ParImm#)
  .map{n -> n + total}
  .list
```

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, let `total` be the checked sum of the `f#ej` over every index `j`, in flow order: if it overflows, the whole flow expression returns that error. Otherwise, if there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej + total` overflows, it returns that error; otherwise it returns the list holding `f#ej + total` at each index `j`.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ xs.add(f#e); }
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat x: xs){ total= total + x; }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat x: xs){ res.add(x + total); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
List[Nat] xs= m.list;
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat x: xs){ total= total + x; }
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(xs.get(j) + total); }
  }.list;
```
With `{xs -> xs.seqFlow.fold({0}, {a, x -> a.aluAddWrap x})}` in place of `{xs -> xs.seqFlow.sum 0}`, the sum can neither throw nor fail to terminate, and the sequential strategy may compute it inside the loop of `f`:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat x= f#e;
  xs.add(x);
  total= total.aluAddWrap(x);
  }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat x: xs){ res.add(x + total); }
return res.list;
```

**example with distinct, on a repeated element**\
```
xs.flow.map f
  .distinct by .filter {::>3}.list
```
with `xs` the list `Lists#(1, 1, 2)`, `ei` its element at index `i`, and `by` an `OrderHashBy[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `i` such that `f#ei`, the key `by#(f#ei)`, or `_KeepNew` on that key throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns, in flow order, the `f#ei` greater than `3` for which `_KeepNew` returns `True`.

The two `f#1` are structurally equivalent, but a `by` whose `.cmp` answers `.lt` to every call keeps both of them.

Sequential strategy:
```
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: xs){
  Nat v= f#e;
  if(!kept.add(v)){ continue; }
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(xs.get(j))); }
  };
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: m){
  if(!kept.add(v)){ continue; }
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism is the same code, with `Mailbox[Nat] m= node(Nat e: xs){ out.send(f#e); };`.

Data parallel, with the filter in tasks of its own:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(xs.get(j))); }
  };
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
Mailbox[Nat] ks= node(Nat v: m){ if(kept.add(v)){ out.send(v); } };
return fork(List[Nat] c: Chunks#ks){
  for(Nat v: c){
    if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
    out.send(v);
    }
  }.list;
```

## Plan

The implementation proceeds in four stages. Stage 1 has the semantics of `Base:Defensive`; from Stage 2 on, every stage has the same semantics, including every error, every misuse error and every call of `by`: two stages differ only as **Execution model vs actual execution behaviour** allows. Each stage keeps the one before it as the oracle for its tests.

### Stage 1: Desugarings

No parallelism: the semantics of `Base:Defensive`, implemented as `Base:DefensiveNaive`. The desugarings of **Signatures** are the implementation, written in Fearless; the core methods are magic following their pseudocode.
Stage 1 has the whole API: every signature of **Signatures** exists in `base`.
`.suppressMisuseExceptions`, `.hintSequential`, `.hintParallel` and `.recoverFlow` record no flag, hint or mode: each sends every element downstream.

The flow object records only the core methods of its chain. The terminal sends each element of the source through one sink per core stage, in flow order; a `Break#` stops the source, and what is sent downstream after it is dropped. Single use is a flag on the flow object, set by the call that consumes it.

`distinct`, and the placement of `mapping`, `set` and `eSet`, are in the runtime.

Tests: every example of this document, as **Tests** under **Implementation pitfalls** describes, under `.suppressMisuseExceptions` where the example throws a misuse error; the integration projects on what a parallel flow promises (order, the error at the smallest index, nothing run after a decision, termination).
Speed is not a goal.

### Before Stage 1: decisions to take and things to fix

1. **Where the shape and the checks live.**\
Options: (a) the flow object keeps the chain as Fearless data (the mode, one step per stage/terminal with its lambdas and the `limit` numbers) and the checks are Fearless over it, with two magic predicates: whether a lambda carries the onlyImmCapture flag and whether two elements of the source are identity equal; (b) chain and checks in the runtime. Recommended: (a). Goes in **Stage 2: Sequential**. Done when the step data type and the two predicates are named there.

2. **Tests as the oracle.**\
**Stage 1: Desugarings** lists them; to define: the harness for a lambda that does not terminate, the harness for a `by` breaking its contract, and the list of the examples of this document as test names, each with the indexes at which its lambdas throw. Done when every example has a test name and the two harnesses exist in `testing.fear` or the integration projects.

### Stage 2: Sequential

Optimised implementations of the sequential strategy, in each base of `BaseOffensiveDefensiveNaive`; in `Base:Offensive`, the misuse errors of **Misuse errors** on top of them.
- The misuse error texts, following the style of the errors of the Frontend.
- One loop per chain, sending each element through the stages with no allocation per element and no JDK stream, as the pseudocode of the sequential strategy; only fences materialise.
- `for` directly after a fence iterates the list of that fence, with no copy.
- A `ToFlow` that a lambda of `flatMap` or `flatMapMut` returns, or that `++` takes, is iterated directly, with no flow object and no copy.
- The rewrites of **Canonical form** and the legal rewrites of **Rewrites of combinations of stages and terminals**.
- The checks run once per terminal, not once per element; the identity check is one `IdentityHashMap` pass over the source.
Tests: every test of Stage 1, each misuse error through `checkNonDetErr`, plus differential tests running random chains, random lambdas (some throwing, some not respecting `by`'s contract) and random sources under Stage 1 and under Stage 2 in `Base:Defensive`, and comparing what they return.
Speed goal: close to a plain Java loop over the same work, and never slower than a JDK sequential stream.

### Stage 3: Parallel dynamic

When the mode allows it, the terminal analyses the chain and picks a parallel strategy at run time, or the sequential strategy of stage 2.
- Data parallelism for the regions of elementwise stages, with the fences as the boundaries between regions.
- Positive speculation calls, executed as part of a stage/terminal running over `read` or `imm` elements (**Over `read` or `imm` elements**).
- Before a `.limit n`, when every stage since the source or the last fence sends at most one element downstream per element received, the tasks run those stages on the next `n - k` elements, with `k` the count of elements the `limit` has received, until `k == n`. Under pipeline parallelism the nodes share only the count left to the `limit`.
- `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max` and `min` run their merge trees as tasks while they receive the elements.
- `for` waits for every task, except when `c.mode == ParImm`, every lambda of the stages carries the onlyImmCapture flag and `c.stages.none({flatMapMut, actorMut, allToAll, ++})`: then the `for` lambda runs in a node as it receives the elements, as in **example with for**.
- `forEach` and `fold` run their lambda on the calling worker as they receive the elements.
- Pipeline parallelism for `actor`, `actorMut`, `scan`, map with context and the placement of `distinct`, `mapping`, `set` and `eSet`, fed by data parallel regions.
- The calls of `kem.key` and `kem.elem` of `mapping`, and of `asImm` of `set` and `eSet`, join the data parallel region before the terminal.
- `.map(ctx, f)` may instead run the calls of `.iso.close` on the state in flow order, and the calls of `f` with data parallelism, each call of `f` with the result of its own call of `.iso.close`.
- Choosing between the strategies by the hints, the size of the source and measurements of the first elements (**Choosing between strategies**).
- The runtime pieces of **Cancellation** and **Workers** under **Implementation pitfalls**.
- A sink drops what is sent through it after the call that owns the sink has returned.
- Open: the sink of `actor` as `mut` or `mutH`.
A reasonable order inside this stage: data parallel regions before consuming terminals over `ParImm` and `ParRead`; short-circuit terminals with positive speculation calls; fences; `ParRepr` and `ParPod`; pipeline parallelism.
Tests: every test of the stages before it run under every strategy the terminal can pick, forced through the hints; tests where lambdas throw at a greater index earlier in time, and where a call after a decision does not terminate; repeated runs of the same flow expression.
Speed goal: benchmarks with CPU bound lambdas that do not allocate, compared with stage 2 at one worker and at every worker.

### Stage 4: Backend integration

When a whole flow expression is written in one place, from the source to the terminal with the lambdas written in place, the backend generates Java methods for it, as the pseudocode of the examples: one for the sequential strategy, one or more for the parallel strategies, and a check at the top choosing between them, on the same information stage 3 uses. Every lambda written in place is inlined, and the state of an actor becomes local variables.
- Inlining the lambdas makes the static cost estimate and the legal rewrites that need to know that a lambda can not throw or fail to terminate (as `{::>3}` over a `Nat` in **sort then filter**) available.
- The misuse errors other than the identity check of `checkPar` are known at compile time: the backend can also report them as it compiles, but the generated code still throws them.
- A flow expression that is not written in one place, as a flow passed to a method, still runs through the library of stage 3.
- A `return` at a `Break#` inside the generated loops is legal where nothing the sequential strategy runs follows the `down.push#` in the same call, as in the finished list of a `flatMap` expansion and in the actor of `limit` itself.
Tests: the pseudocode of the examples as the expected output of the generator for their flow expressions; every test of the stages before it with each flow expression written in one place and passed through a method, comparing the two.

### Canonical form

Before generating code, a backend can rewrite the chain into a canonical form, with these legal rewrites (**Rewrites of combinations of stages and terminals**).
A 1-1 stage is a stage of `OneToOne` (**Misuse errors**), or an `actor` or `actorMut` whose lambda calls `down.push#` exactly once and returns `Continue#` on every path.
- `.s .limit n` is `.limit n .s`, for every 1-1 stage `s`.
- `.limit n .limit m` is `.limit(n.min(m))`.
- `.map f .map g` is `.map{x -> g#(f#x)}`, and `.filter p .filter q` is `.filter{x -> p#x && {q#x}}`.
- `.limit n` right after a list source of known size is the source cut to its first `n` elements.

The canonical form moves each `limit` as close to the source as these rewrites allow, and fuses what is left.

### Rewrites of combinations of stages and terminals

Each entry below is a combination of stages and terminals with a rewrite that is legal, or that looks legal and is not.
A rewrite is legal when the whole flow expression returns the same for every lambda, including those that throw or do not terminate, and makes the calls of `by` of `_Sort`, `_Max`, `_Collapse` and `_KeepNew`.

**scan then limit**\
`l.flow.scan(0, h).limit 3 .list`\
with `h` of type `read F[Nat,Nat,Nat]`.

Let `s0` be `h#(0, e0)` and `sj` be `h#(s(j-1), ej)`. If there is a smallest index `j` among `0`, `1` and `2` such that computing `sj` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns. Otherwise it returns `Lists#(s0, s1, s2)`.

Legal: `scan` is a 1-1 stage, so `.scan(acc, h) .limit n` is `.limit n .scan(acc, h)`, the limit moves on to the source, and the element at index `3` is never processed in either order. Written as `.scan(acc, h) .limit n`, the shape throws a deceptive shape error: this rewrite applies under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`.

Sequential strategy:
```
Nat s= 0;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l.take(3)){
  s= h#(s, e);
  res.add(s);
  }
return res.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Nat s= 0;
return node(Nat e: l.take(3)){ s= h#(s, e); out.send(s); }.list;
```

**scan then last**\
`l.flow.scan(0, h).last`

With `sj` as above, if there is a smallest index `j` such that computing `sj` throws or does not terminate, it returns what that call returns; otherwise it returns `Opts#s7`.

Legal: the calls of `h` are those of a fold, in the same order; only the last accumulator is kept, and nothing is materialised.
```
Nat s= 0;
for(Nat e: l){ s= h#(s, e); }
if(l.isEmpty){ return Opt#; }
return Opts#s;
```

**max then limit, sort then last**\
`l.flow.map f .max by .limit 2 .list` and `l.flow.map f .sort by .last`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

For `.max by .limit 2`: if there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns. Otherwise, if some calls of `by` made by the max tree throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the tree makes before the others returns. Otherwise it returns the first two, in flow order, of the elements tied for the maximum.

Legal: a merge of the max tree compares only the front elements of its two blocks, so capping every block at `2` elements changes no call.
```
List[Nat] maxMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  Ord o= by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0));
  if(o.isGt){ return a; }
  if(o.isLt){ return b; }
  return (a ++ b).take(k);
  }
```
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Max#(vs, by, 2);
```
where `_Max#(vs, by, k)` is the tree of `_Max#` with this merge.

`.sort by .last` throws a deceptive shape error, suggesting `.max by .last`. Under `.suppressMisuseExceptions` it is the greatest element under `by`, the one with the greatest index among ties. A merge tree keeping only the last element of each block makes one call per merge:
```
List[Nat] lastMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by){
  if(by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0)).isGt){ return a; }
  return b;
  }
```
Not legal: it makes other calls of `by` than `_Sort`, and for a `by` not respecting its contract it may return a different element. Under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`, `.sort by .last` runs the whole `_Sort`.

**distinct then first**\
`l.flow.map f .distinct by .first`\
with `by` an `OrderHashBy[Nat]`.

If `f#e0`, the key `by#(f#e0)`, or `_KeepNew` on that key throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns. Otherwise it returns `Opts#(f#e0)`.
```
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat v= f#e;
  kept.add(v);
  return Opts#v;
  }
return Opt#;
```
Looks legal, is not: skipping the calls of `by` on `f#e0`.

**sort then filter**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .filter p .list`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]` and `p` a `read F[Nat,Bool]`.

Throws a deceptive shape error; the results and the first pseudocode below are what runs under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns. Otherwise, if some calls of `by` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the sorting process makes before the others returns. Otherwise let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index: if there is a smallest `k` such that `p#vk` throws or does not terminate, it returns what `p#vk` returns; otherwise it returns, in that order, the `vk` for which `p#vk` completes successfully with `True`.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: _Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){
  if(!p#v){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Looks legal, is not: `.filter p .sort by` sorts fewer elements, so it makes other calls of `by` than `_Sort`, also when `p` can neither throw nor fail to terminate, as `{::>3}` over a `Nat`. The rewritten loop would be:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat v= f#e;
  if(v > 3){ vs.add(v); }
  }
return _Sort#(vs, by, vs.size);
```
The pseudocode of `.filter p .sort by` is the loop above with `p#v` in place of `v > 3`.
Under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`, running `p` early without changing the calls of `by` is still possible, as in **example with elementwise work after a fence**.

**sort then sort**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by1 .sort by2 .list`\
with `by1` and `by2` of type `OrderBy[Nat]`.

The second sort receives the output of the first, and each makes its own calls; the result is the output of the second sort, or what the first call that throws or does not terminate returns, in the order of the semantics of **example with a fence**.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Sort#(_Sort#(vs, by1, vs.size), by2, vs.size);
```
Throws a deceptive shape error; the result and the pseudocode above are what runs under `.suppressMisuseExceptions`.
Looks legal, is not: the second sort is stable, so for comparators respecting their contract the result is ordered by `by2`, with ties ordered by `by1`, as one `.sort(by2.then by1)`; but the calls of `by1` differ, and a comparator not respecting its contract gives a different order.
The pseudocode of `.sort(by2.then by1)`:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Sort#(vs, by2.then by1, vs.size);
```

