None of those stages can be dropped.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Sort#(_Sort#(vs, by, vs.size), by, vs.size);
```
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Sort#(vs, by, vs.size).size;
```
```
Opt[Nat] res= Opt#;
for(Nat e: l){ res= Opts#(f#e); }
return res;
```

**flatMap then flatMap**\
`l.flow.flatMap f .flatMap g .limit 3 .list`\
with `f` and `g` of type `read F[Nat, read List[Nat]]`.

The whole flow expression processes the elements of `l` in flow order; for each element `e`, it computes `f#e` and, for each element `a` of it in order, `g#a`, sending its elements downstream. It stops right after the call of `g` whose list holds the third element sent downstream: the first call that throws or does not terminate before that decides what it returns; otherwise it returns the first three elements sent downstream.
```
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  for(Nat a: f#e){
    for(Nat x: g#a){
      res.add(x);
      if(res.size == 3){ return res.list; }
      }
    }
  }
return res.list;
```
Looks legal, is not: `.flatMap f .flatMap g` looks associative, as `.flatMap{x -> f#x .seqFlow.flatMap g .list}`, but the lambda of `.flatMap` builds its whole list before sending any element of it downstream. The candidate calls `g` on every element of `f#e0`, even after the third element is sent downstream.
With `f#0` returning `Lists#(1, 2)`, `g#1` returning `Lists#(10, 11, 12)` and `g#2` throwing, the whole flow expression returns `Lists#(10, 11, 12)`, and the candidate throws.
With `.list` directly after the two stages, the calls and their order are the same, and the rewrite is legal.

**a fence before a short-circuit terminal**\
`ps.seqFlow.map{p -> Block#(p.birthday, p)} ++ List[mut Person]{} .first`\
with `ps` a `mut List[mut Person]`.

If `ps` is empty, the whole flow expression returns `Opt#`. Otherwise, if there is a smallest index `j` such that `pj.birthday` throws or does not terminate, it returns what that call returns. Otherwise every person in `ps` is one year older, and it returns `Opts#p0`, as `read`.

`.first` is `.limit 1 .getOpt`. Without the `++`, the `map` would run on `p0` only; with it, it runs on every person.
```
for(mut Person p: ps){ p.birthday; }
for(mut Person p: ps){ return Opts#p; }
return Opt#;
```
The `map` returns its own element, so the list the fence materialises is `ps` itself.

**two max**\
`l.flow.map f .max by1 .max by2 .list`\
with `by1` and `by2` of type `OrderBy[Nat]`.

Throws no deceptive shape error.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by1` made by the max tree throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the tree makes before the others returns; every call of `by2` comes after every call of `by1`, since the `max by2` stage receives nothing until the `max by1` stage has finished.\
Otherwise the `max by1` stage keeps the elements tied for the maximum under `by1`, in flow order; the `max by2` stage keeps, among those, the ones tied for the maximum under `by2`. When every call of `by1` and `by2` completes successfully, this is one `max` under the order that compares with `by1` and breaks ties with `by2`.
That single pass is not a legal replacement: it calls `by2` on two elements as soon as `by1` ties them, and an element after both in flow order may beat both under `by1`. The `max by2` stage never receives such elements, so a call of `by2` on them that throws or does not terminate changes the result.

What is legal:
- The `max by1` stage materialises only its current leading run, not the whole flow, and the `max by2` stage runs on that run alone once the flow ends. With the max tree, the `max by1` stage runs with data parallelism too.
- The single pass with positive speculation calls, allowed in `ParImm` and `ParRead`: `by2` runs on the current leading run as the elements are received, and its errors are held until the `max by1` stage finishes and confirms the run; calls on elements that left the run are discarded, or cancelled through `Yield#`.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return _Max#(_Max#(vs, by1), by2);
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
MaxTree[Nat] s= MaxTree#by1;
for(Nat v: m){ s.add(v); }
return _Max#(s.list, by2);
```
The `max by2` stage runs only on the leading run `s.list` returns, so no call of `by2` is made on an element the `max by1` stage drops.

### Choosing between strategies

**Choosing by cost:**\
A backend may choose a strategy with any information, also information that changes between runs or during one run:
- Static estimate: after inlining, the body of each stage is visible code. `v > 3` is one comparison, with no call to unknown code, no loop and no allocation: it costs less than sending the element to a task, so it runs in the loop of the stage before it. A body calling an abstract lambda such as `f`, or a method with loops or recursion, has an unknown cost.
- Hints: the user states the cost.
- Measurement: the generated code holds one version per strategy, and the runtime times the first elements of each stage, then picks a version and adapts the chunk sizes as it goes. `allTheCoresBusy`, the extreme case of this, checks at the start of the terminal whether every worker is busy, and if so picks the sequential strategy.
- Profiles: measurements of previous runs, stored per flow expression, pick the version at the next run.

**Memoisation by identity:**\
`orders.flow.map{o -> o.product}.map{p -> Price#p}.list`\
with `orders` an `imm List[imm Order]` where many orders share one `imm Product` object, and `Price#p` an expensive computation returning a `Nat`.

A stage may keep an `IdentityHashMap` from element to result, per task or shared between tasks, when the element is `imm`, the lambda of the stage carries the onlyImmCapture flag, and the result is `imm`: the second `map` above can.

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityMap[Product, Nat] cache= IdentityMap#;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Order o: orders){
  Product p= o.product;
  Opt[Nat] hit= cache.get(p);
  if(hit.isSome){ res.add(hit!); continue; }
  Nat x= Price#p;
  cache.put(p, x);
  res.add(x);
  }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel, with one cache per task:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(orders.size)){
  IdentityMap[Product, Nat] cache= IdentityMap#;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Product p= orders.get(j).product;
    Opt[Nat] hit= cache.get(p);
    if(hit.isSome){ out.send(hit!); continue; }
    Nat x= Price#p;
    cache.put(p, x);
    out.send(x);
    }
  }.list;
```
A call of `Price` that throws puts nothing in the cache, so the error is thrown at the smallest index where the sequential strategy without the cache throws it.

### Implementation pitfalls

Each entry is a way an implementation can look correct, pass tests of values and order, and still break the semantics above.

**JDK streams:**\
No JDK stream, sequential or parallel, can be the implementation of a flow, nor its consumer side:
- `count()` returns the size of a source whose size is known without running the stages, so `map f .size` never calls `f`.
- A parallel stream throws the error that happens first in time, not the one at the smallest index.
- Every terminal of a parallel stream waits for all its tasks, so a task after the decision that does not terminate makes `findFirst` not terminate.
- A parallel `forEach`, or a collector writing into one `LinkedHashMap`, runs the consumer on several workers: the order is lost, and elements too.
- The iterator of a stream buffers whole `flatMap` expansions.
- `min` and `max` return one element, not every element tied for the maximum; `distinct` uses `equals` and `hashCode`, not an `OrderHashBy`; `limit(0)` returns the empty stream; `Stream.concat` is not a fence.
- `List.sort` (TimSort, also behind `EList.sortAnd`) compares an element with one before it in flow order, in its own order of calls, which no parallel sort can reproduce, and whether it throws `IllegalArgumentException` for a comparator breaking its contract depends on those calls: a parallel strategy would detect a different set of broken comparators. Sorting uses the merge tree in every strategy, with no detection.
- `Collectors.toMap` and `Map.put` keep one value for a repeated key, where `mapping` throws.

**Waiting and buffering:**
- Joining every task before the terminal returns: a speculative call that does not terminate then makes the whole flow expression not terminate where the sequential strategy returns.
- Reading the output of a task only once the whole task has finished: the terminal waits for the elements of the task after its decision, and one of them may not terminate.
- Feeding a trickling terminal through an iterator or any other buffering, when one element received becomes many elements, as a `flatMap` expansion or an `actor` sending two elements downstream: the stages after the expansion run on all of it before the terminal receives its first element, so an error of a stage at a greater index is thrown instead of the one of the terminal at a smaller index.

**Cancellation:**
- `Yield#` cancels by throwing a Java exception that is not an `Info`: no Fearless code can catch it. Every place of the runtime that catches Java exceptions on behalf of Fearless code must let it through: `System.try` (`CapTry`), which otherwise turns it into an `Info` and lets the cancelled call go on, and the memo caches, which otherwise store it and throw it to every caller from then on. A memo body cancelled halfway leaves no entry.
- `Yield#` has to work wherever user code runs: data parallel tasks, nodes, the calling worker running the terminal, and nested flows.

**Workers:**
- Virtual threads are never preempted while computing: tasks running user code on virtual threads can occupy every carrier, and the threads coordinating them (the one sending the outputs to the terminal, the one cancelling) then never run. Tasks running user code use platform threads, as the common `ForkJoinPool` does.
- A flow inside the lambda of a stage (a nested flow) runs on a worker. A worker waiting for a nested flow must not run unrelated queued tasks whole while it waits, as `ForkJoinTask.join` may: they can run elements after a decision. When every worker is busy, the calling worker runs the task it is waiting for itself, element by element, so the flow makes progress even when every worker is stuck in a call that does not terminate; this generalises `allTheCoresBusy`.
- A task that is no longer needed may still be running when the whole flow expression returns. It reads only `imm` values when its lambdas carry the onlyImmCapture flag; otherwise it may read, as `read`, objects the caller mutates from then on. Its result is discarded whatever it reads, but in Java it may meet a data race, and may then throw or not terminate: the result is still discarded, and the cost is its worker.

**Measuring:**
- Every `Nat` above 255 is a new heap object, so a lambda doing arithmetic on `Nat`s is bound by allocation, and allocation scales with memory bandwidth, not with workers. Benchmarks of parallel strategies burn time with arithmetic that allocates nothing, such as `Byte` arithmetic, or they measure the allocator.

**Tests:**\
A test checking that the values and their order are right does not test a flow. Every behaviour above needs a test in which some lambdas throw, or do not terminate, at chosen indexes, and in which `by` does not respect its contract; the examples of this document are the first such tests. A test for non termination uses a lambda that loops without `Yield#`, and a timeout in the test runner. Tests over `ParRepr` and `ParPod` check which elements were mutated after the flow returns, not only that each mutation is complete.

## Close calls

Shapes that throw no deceptive shape error:
```
.sort by .findFirst p | .sort by .filter p .first | .sort by .any p  //the order decides which elements the search visits
.sort by .limit k                        //the order of the elements kept is read
.max by1 .max by2 | .min by1 .max by2    //the second selects among what the first keeps
.map f .map g | .filter p .filter q      //p is often a guard q relies on
.sort by .set(..) | .sort by .eSet(..) | .sort by .mapping(..)  //the order decides which element of a class is kept
.sort by1 .sortDistinct by2 | .sort by1 .distinct by2 | .sortDistinct by1 .sortDistinct by2
.sortDistinct by1 .sort by2              //the first also drops repeated elements
.distinct by .filter p | .sortDistinct by .filter p
.map f .last
.peek f .size | .map(ctx, g) .size | .peek(ctx, f) .size
.distinct by1 .distinct by2 | .max by1 .sort by2  //pointless only for equal arguments, not part of the shape
.limit n .isEmpty | .limit n .first | .++ b .isEmpty | .++ b .first  //pointless only for n >= 1 or a non empty b
.max by .first | .distinct by .last | .distinct by .size | .distinct by .getOpt
.flatMapMut f .map g | .flatMapMut f .list | .actorMut(s, f) .distinct by
